- 4.1 Simulation with inverted-U power valley\n   - 4.2 Controlled SFT contamination on Qwen3.5-9B and Gemma-4-31B\n   - 4.3 Theoretical power analysis\n\n5. **When Binary Detection Succeeds**\n   - 5.1 Extreme contamination (Gemini-1.5-Pro, lz*=+34.75)\n   - 5.2 Leaderboard audit (12 models, only Gemini flagged)\n   - 5.3 Domain-level analysis\n\n6. **Attempts to Overcome the Binary Bottleneck**\n   - 6.1 Multi-statistic fusion lz^m (+0.04 AUROC, negligible)\n   - 6.2 4PL model specification effects (variance inflation 1.34)\n\n7. **The Detection Frontier**\n   - 7.1 Method comparison: IRT-FIT (0.63 AUROC) vs ConStat (0.72) vs Min-K%++ (0.81) at 15%\n   - 7.2 Post-RL signal destruction (>88% signal loss after GRPO)\n\n8. **Discussion** — Information-theoretic bottleneck, inverted-U as impossibility, practical value for extreme cases, implications for evaluation infrastructure\n\n9. **Conclusion**\n\n## Related Work Cited (9 references)\n1. Snijders (2001) — lz* statistic, Psychometrika\n2. Zhang et al. (2024) — Min-K%++, ICLR 2024\n3. Shi et al. (2024) — MIA methods, ICLR 2024\n4. Oren et al. (2024) — Time Travel probing, ICLR 2024 Spotlight\n5. Dekoninck et al. (2024) — ConStat, NeurIPS 2024\n6. Anonymous (2026) — PSN-IRT, AAAI 2026 Oral\n7. Hong, Lin & Cheng (2021) — Multiscale lz^m, Applied Psychological Measurement\n8. Wang et al. (2025) — Post-RL contamination, arXiv\n9. Anonymous (2026) — CoDeC, ICLR 2026\n\n## Key Claims of Novelty\n1. First to investigate contamination detection at the minimal-information operating point (binary responses only)\n2. Two-sided detection framework for person-fit (detecting both under-fitting and over-consistency)\n3. Discovery of the inverted-U power curve / \"power valley\" phenomenon\n4. Detection frontier concept — systematic mapping of information access to detection power\n5. Quantification of the information-theoretic gap (binary needs ~42K items vs logits ~4.2K for 80% power at 15%)\n6. First controlled SFT contaminatio